\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Kelley's flat curves}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The system of
\citet{(3.2.1), §3.2, p. 124}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} is
##{\dot x=x^{2},\qquad \dot y=-y.}
A solution with value #{(x_{0},y_{0})} at time #{0} is
##{x(t)=\frac{x_{0}}{1-tx_{0}},\qquad y(t)=y_{0}e^{-t},}
on the time interval that contains #{0} and on which
#{tx_{0}\neq 1}. When #{x_{0}\neq 0}, eliminating the time
gives the graphs
##{y=\bigl(y_{0}e^{-1/x_{0}}\bigr)e^{1/x}.}
\citet{p. 125}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} records these formulas.
For #{x<0} every such graph reaches the origin, and every derivative
of #{e^{1/x}} vanishes there. For #{x>0} the #{x}-axis is the only
solution curve that reaches the origin.}

\p{Join any one of those graphs in the left half plane to the positive
#{x}-axis. The resulting curve is a #{C^{\infty}}
[center manifold](fnip-0059) tangent to the #{x}-axis, and the
velocity along it is #{\dot x=x^{2}}. The #{x}-axis is the only
analytic [center manifold](fnip-0059) of this equilibrium. The
drawing shows several of the curves, after Figure 3.2.1 on that page.}

